  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 453 AM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nDry and Warm Start to the Week:\n\nA dry airmass will spread across North and Central GA early next \nweek within deep northwest flow on the backside of a mid-level \ntrough. With PWAT values dropping below 1.5\" and a mid-level ridge \nextending over the area from the southern Plains, rain chances will \nbe hard to come by through Wednesday. With limited cloud cover and a \nwarm airmass over the region, heat concerns will likely return. High \ntemperatures will rise to the low to mid 90s each day, with heat \nindex values in the triple digits. \n\nConfidence in the forecast drops off from mid to late next week due \nto a spread in guidance on how the mid-level pattern evolves. The \nscenario most supported by the latest guidance is a frontal boundary \nmoving through the area in the Wednesday-Thursday timeframe, with an \neven drier airmass spreading over the region in its wake. A smaller \ncamp of guidance has the trough over the Eastern Seaboard building \nback west, which would allow a humid airmass (and rain chances) to \nmove back in across our area. For now, leaning with the former \nsolution given the growing number of model members trending that \nway. If this were to come to fruition, it would be a pretty dry week \nfor late July standards, but with typical summer heat.",
